  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 242 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nMid-level pieces of energy continue to swing around the \nnorthern periphery of a ridge centered across TX, resulting in \nlow chances for scattered rain showers continuing into Saturday\nand possibly Sunday. As we progress further into the weekend, \nsome of the better chances (50%) move south and east reaching \nportions of central/east-central MO. Similar to Friday, mostly \nlight rain showers are forecast as there is less than a 20% \nchance for thunderstorms. The better instability (MUCAPE >1,000 \nJ/kg) stays to the west across the Great Plains this weekend, \nwith LREF mean MUCAPE values staying below 200 J/kg across the \nentire area. By Tuesday, the large-scale pattern begins to shift\nas a longwave trough swings into the Intermountain West and the\nsouthern ridge into the Deep South. This places our area in \nsoutherly flow leading to warm/moist advection that will support\nincreasing chances for more widespread showers along with \nthunderstorms. The LREF illustrates this potential well as \nprobabilities for precipitation >0.50\" reaches 50% for western \nportions of the area, indicating the increasing chances for more\nwidespread beneficial rain. With southerly flow in place, \ntemperatures IQRs steadily increase next week to values centered\njust above average. However, these IQR spreads increase to 10 \ndegrees by Wednesday and Thursday, showing the potential for a \ncold front behind next weeks system. This reveals a return to \nnear or slightly below normal temperatures are possible by the \nend of next week.",
